Subject: [PATCH] Fix HashPSQ.insert tie-break for equal priorities

Synthetic restatement of upstream commit c107f382d7c93692e0bb64140c4f6823f25d76e1
("HashPSQ.valid (and fix bug found using valid...)") narrowed to the
insert priority-comparison guard. Reverse-applying this patch installs
the bug: when two keys hash to the same bucket and are inserted with
equal priority, the buggy `p' <= p` guard keeps the first-inserted item
as the bucket head, so `findMin` reports the wrong key/value (the
modern fix uses a strict `<` plus a key tie-breaker).
---
 src/Data/HashPSQ/Internal.hs | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/Data/HashPSQ/Internal.hs b/src/Data/HashPSQ/Internal.hs
--- a/src/Data/HashPSQ/Internal.hs
+++ b/src/Data/HashPSQ/Internal.hs
@@ -181,7 +181,7 @@ insert k p v (HashPSQ ipsq) =
         | k' == k                       =
             -- Tricky: p might have less priority than an item in 'os'.
             Just (mkBucket k p v os)
-        | p' <= p                       =
+        | p' < p || (p == p' && k' < k) =
             Just (p', B k' v' (OrdPSQ.insert k  p  v  os))
         | OrdPSQ.member k os            =
             -- This is a bit tricky: k might already be present in 'os' and we
